Article 2. In the following civil dispute cases, where a party uses the well-known status of a trademark as a factual basis, and the People's Court considers it truly necessary in light of the specific circumstances of the case, it shall make a determination as to whether the trademark concerned is well known:
(1) an action for infringement of trademark right brought on the ground that the provisions of Article 13 of the Trademark Law are violated;
(2) an action for infringement of trademark right or unfair competition brought on the ground that an enterprise name is identical or similar to its well-known trademark;
(3) an action involving a defense or counterclaim that conforms to the provisions of Article 6 of this Interpretation.

Article 5. Where a party asserts that a trademark is well known, it shall, in light of the specific circumstances of the case, provide the following evidence to prove that, at the time when the alleged act of trademark infringement or unfair competition occurred, its trademark was already well known:
(1) the market share, sales territory, profits and taxes, etc. of the goods using the trademark;
(2) the duration of continuous use of the trademark;
(3) the manner, duration, extent, capital input, and territorial scope of the publicity or promotional activities for the trademark;
(4) the records of the trademark having previously been protected as a well-known trademark;
(5) the market reputation enjoyed by the trademark;
(6) other facts proving that the trademark was already well known.
The time, scope, manner, etc. of trademark use referred to in the preceding paragraph include the circumstances of continuous use prior to the approval and registration of the trademark.
With respect to evidence such as the length of trademark use, industry ranking, market survey reports, market value assessment reports, and whether the trademark has previously been recognized as a famous trademark, the People's Court shall conduct an objective and comprehensive examination in conjunction with other evidence for determining whether the trademark is well known.

Article 10. Where the plaintiff requests to prohibit the defendant from using, on non-similar goods, a trademark or enterprise name that is identical or similar to the plaintiff's registered well-known trademark, the People's Court shall, in light of the specific circumstances of the case, adjudge after comprehensively considering the following factors:
(1) the degree of distinctiveness of the well-known trademark;
(2) the degree of awareness of the well-known trademark among the relevant public of the goods using the accused trademark or enterprise name;
(3) the degree of correlation between the goods using the well-known trademark and the goods using the accused trademark or enterprise name;
(4) other relevant factors.

Article 11. Where the registered trademark used by the defendant violates the provisions of Article 13 of the Trademark Law by reproducing, imitating, or translating the plaintiff's well-known trademark, and constitutes infringement of trademark right, the People's Court shall, upon the plaintiff's request, adjudge according to law to prohibit the defendant from using the trademark; provided that, if the defendant's registered trademark falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall not support the plaintiff's request:
(1) the period for requesting invalidation as prescribed in paragraph 1 of Article 45 of the Trademark Law has already expired;
(2) at the time the defendant filed the application for registration, the plaintiff's trademark was not well known.
